The purpose of the Facade pattern is simple:

  Provide a single, simplified interface to a complex subsystem.

Think of it as a "front desk" that hides all the complexity behind one easy-to-use API.
================================================================
Real-Life Example

      Imagine you're checking into a hotel.
      
      Without a facade:

          Customer
             │
             ├── Reception
             ├── Room Service
             ├── Housekeeping
             ├── Restaurant
             ├── Laundry
             ├── Billing
             └── Security


  You would need to contact each department individually.

      With a facade:

          Customer
                │
                ▼
          Hotel Reception (Facade)
                │
                ├── Housekeeping
                ├── Billing
                ├── Restaurant
                ├── Laundry
                └── Security

The customer only interacts with Reception.
=================================================================================
Problem

            Suppose you're building an E-commerce Checkout.
            
            A checkout process requires:
            
                      Inventory Service
                      Payment Service
                      Discount Service
                      Shipping Service
                      Notification Service
                      Invoice Service
            
            Without a facade:

                    CheckoutController                    
                       │
                       ├── InventoryService
                       ├── PaymentService
                       ├── ShippingService
                       ├── DiscountService
                       ├── InvoiceService
                       └── NotificationService

The controller becomes tightly coupled to every subsystem.
              inventoryService.checkStock();
              
              discountService.applyCoupon();
              
              paymentService.pay();
              
              shippingService.createShipment();
              
              invoiceService.generate();
              
              notificationService.sendEmail();

This violates the Single Responsibility Principle and makes maintenance difficult.
==============================================================================

Solution

Create one class: CheckoutFacade
The controller now calls: checkoutFacade.checkout(order);
Internally:
              CheckoutFacade
                    │
                    ├── Inventory
                    ├── Discount
                    ├── Payment
                    ├── Shipping
                    ├── Invoice
                    └── Notification
The client never sees the complexity.
=================================================================================
UML Diagram
                            Client
                               │
                               ▼
                        CheckoutFacade
                  ┌─────----───┼─────-----------────┐
                  ▼            ▼                    ▼
           Inventory       Payment               Shipping
                  ▼            ▼                    ▼
           Discount         Invoice           Notification

====================================================================================

Java Example:


            Facade
            public class CheckoutFacade {
            
                private final InventoryService inventory =
                        new InventoryService();
            
                private final PaymentService payment =
                        new PaymentService();
            
                private final ShippingService shipping =
                        new ShippingService();
            
                private final NotificationService notification =
                        new NotificationService();
            
                public void checkout(String product){
            
                    if(inventory.isAvailable(product)){
            
                        payment.pay();
            
                        shipping.ship();
            
                        notification.sendEmail();
                    }
                }

          }

Client
        public class Main {
        
            public static void main(String[] args) {
        
                CheckoutFacade facade =
                        new CheckoutFacade();
        
                facade.checkout("Laptop");
            }
        }



The client knows only one class: CheckoutFacade

The controller has only one dependency.
